"Licensing Overview | U.S. Copyright Office (https://www.copyright.gov/licensing/)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.copyright.gov/licensing/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 205\nL0:   * Research\nL1: \nL2: Research\nL3: \nL4: Resources\nL5: \nL6:     * cite0†Search Copyright Records: Copyright Public Records Portal L7: \nL8: Services\nL9: # Licensing Overview\nL10: \nL11: Note: As of July 1, 2025, the Licensing Section requires electronic filing for all cable and satellite statements of account (SOAs). The section will only accept submissions using the forms provided on its website in their native Excel format. Submission by mail will not be accepted. New SA1-2E and SA3E forms take effect with the 2025/2 accounting period. For more information on changes to the filing process and SOA forms, please read our updated cite1†eSOA FAQs .\nL12: Note: Effective March 31, 2025, the Licensing Section requires the use of Pay.gov to make all electronic royalty and filing fee payments for the current filing year and period (2026/1). For more information on how to make statutory license royalty payments, please read cite2†Circular 74 and view the cite3†tutorial slide deck .\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Please Read: Message addressing on-site cable filers UPDATED\nL17: ## Licensing Section Has Ceased Returns of Electronically Submitted Statements\nL18: \nL19: Beginning with the 2023/1 accounting period, the Licensing Section no longer returns signed PDFs of received electronic statements of account (eSOAs). These statements are available on the Licensing Section’s webpage, (cite4†Section 111 Statements of Account Electronically Filed with the Copyright Office ), and are updated weekly as the section processes the statements.\nL20: ## Statutory Licensing Fiduciary Assets Financial Statements and Independent Auditors’ Report\nL21: The Library of Congress contracted with an independent public accounting firm, Sikich CPA LLC, to conduct an audit of the financial statements prepared for the fiduciary assets administered by the Copyright Office Licensing Section.\nL22: The purpose of this independent audit was to provide an opinion on the fairness of the financial statements in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, and to report on internal control over financial reporting and compliance with applicable laws and regulations. For more information on the results of the audit, visit the link below.\nL23: cite5†FY 2024 Report L24: ## EFT Information\nL25: \nL26: All statutory license royalty fee payments must be paid by Electronic Funds Transfer. For more information, please read:\nL27: \nL28: ### Payments\nL29: \nL30:   * cite6†How to Make Statutory License Royalty EFT Payments Using Pay.gov L31:   * cite7†Pay.gov FAQ L32:   * cite8†EFT Remittance Advice Template L33: \nL34: ### Refunds and Disbursements\nL35: \nL36:   * cite9†EFT Form L37:   * cite10†Change to EFT payment process L38: ## Resources\nL39: \nL40:   * cite11†Technical Changes to the Regulations Governing Licensing L41:   * cite12†Satellite Television Community Protection and Promotion Act of 2019 L42:   * cite13†Licensing Documents L43:   * cite14†Search LDS†licensing.copyright.gov L44:   * cite15†Licensing Fees L45:   * cite16†Investigate the Status of a Work L46:   * cite17†Licensing FAQs L47:   * cite18†Contact Us L48: The Licensing Section is responsible for helping to administer the various statutory licenses and similar provisions, including: secondary transmissions of radio and television programs by cable and satellite systems; making and distributing phonorecords of nondramatic musical works; and importing, manufacturing, and distributing digital audio recording devices or media.\nL49: In general the section deducts its operating costs from the royalty fees collected and invests the balance in interest-bearing securities with the U.S. Treasury for later distribution to copyright owners. The section also collects filing fees to cover part of the costs in administering the cable and satellite licenses. For further information read cite19†Circular 75 , The Licensing Section of the Copyright Office.\nL50: Visit the cite20†Copyright Royalty Board website†www.crb.gov for information on determination of rates and terms for the copyright statutory licenses and for determinations on distribution of statutory license royalties collected by the Copyright Office.\nL51: ## Types of Licenses\nL52: \nL53: ### Cable cite21†Image: Cable icon L54: \nL55: cite22†Section 111 – Statutory License for Secondary Transmissions by Cable Systems\nL56: \nL57: ### Music cite23†Image: Music icon L58: \nL59: cite24†Section 112 – Statutory License for Making Ephemeral Recordings\nL60: \nL61: cite24†Section 114 – Statutory License for the public performance of Sound Recordings by Means of a Digital Audio Transmission\nL62: \nL63: cite25†Section 115 – Compulsory License for Making and Distributing Phonorecords\nL64: ### Satellite cite26†Image: Satellite Dish icon L65: \nL66: cite27†Section 119 – Statutory License for Secondary Transmissions for Satellite Carriers\nL67: \nL68: cite28†Section 122 – Statutory License for Secondary Transmissions by Satellite Carriers for Local Retransmissions\nL69: \nL70: ### Other Licenses\nL71: \nL72: cite29†Section 1003 – Statutory Obligation for Distribution of Digital Audio Recording Devices and Media\nL73: ## Statutory Licensing Fiduciary Assets Audited Financial Statements\nL74: \nL75: [Button: Select Report Year]\nL76: \nL77: cite30†Fiscal Year 2025 cite5†Fiscal Year 2024 cite31†Fiscal Year 2023 cite32†Fiscal Year 2022 cite33†Fiscal Year 2021 cite34†Fiscal Year 2020 cite35†Fiscal Year 2019 cite36†Fiscal Year 2018 cite37†Fiscal Year 2017 L78: ## Distributions and Financial Statements\nL79: \nL80:   * cite38†Report of Receipts L81:   * cite39†Balance Sheet L82:   * cite40†Statement of Financing L83:   * cite41†Distribution Fund Chart L84:   * cite42†Year-Over-Year Growth in the Copyright Royalty Funds L85: \nL86: cite43†Image L87: \nL88: ## The Licensing Connection Newsletter\nL89: \nL90: ### Current Issue\nL91: \nL92:   * cite44†Spring-Summer 2026 L93: \nL94: ### Previous Issues\nL95: \nL96:   * cite45†Fall-Winter 2025 L97:   * cite46†Spring-Summer 2025 L98:   * cite47†Fall-Winter 2024 L99:   * cite48†Spring-Summer 2024 L100:   * cite49†Fall-Winter 2023 L101:   * cite50†Spring-Summer 2023 L102: ## Survey\nL103: \nL104: To help us serve you better, please participate in an anonymous cite51†website survey†www.surveymonkey.com .\nL105: # Final rule making technical changes to the regulations governing licensing\nL106: The Copyright Office has issued a final rule making technical changes to the regulations governing licensing. These amendments improve the quality of the Office’s regulations by updating cross-references, replacing outdated terminology, reflecting changes to Office practices, eliminating expired or obsolete provisions, and correcting nonsubstantive errors.\nL107: Of note, cite52†Section 201.6(a) regarding payments was amended and Section 201.9 regarding the recordation of agreements between copyright owners and public broadcasting entities pursuant to 17 U.S.C. § 118 was removed and reserved.\nL108: [Button: Close]\nL109: # Change regarding electronic funds transfer (EFT) payments\nL110: The Library of Congress is adopting a federal best practice by transferring its payment processes to the U.S. Treasury. As of April 3rd, 2017, the U.S. Treasury began issuing all checks and electronic funds transfer (EFT) payments on behalf of the Library. As a result of this change the U.S. Treasury may subject some payments to reductions under the Treasury Offset Program, through which the Treasury collects delinquent debt owed to federal and state agencies.\nL111: For frequently asked questions about the Treasury Offset Program, visit cite53†https://fiscal.treasury.gov/top/faqs-for-the-public.html†fiscal.treasury.gov .\nL112: [Button: Close]\nL113: # How to Make Statutory License Royalty EFT Payments Using Pay.gov\nL114: \nL115: cite2†EFT Payments Using Pay.gov (Circular 74) L116: ###### To begin the EFT process, remitters need to select the Pay.gov form that corresponds to their type of royalty payment (cable, satellite, or DART) and the payment method.\nL117:   * Go to the Pay.gov website, cite54†www.Pay.gov†www.pay.gov .\nL118:   * Click on See All Forms and select “Library of Congress (LOC): Copyright Office” or enter Copyright Office in the search bar.\nL119:   * Click on U.S. Copyright Office (Cable, DART, Satellite). Select the type of royalty payment and the method of payment:\nL120:   * Cable companies should click either\nL121:     * » Library of Congress Cable Royalty Payment (credit card, debit card, or ACH debit payments) or\nL122:     * » Library of Congress Cable Royalty Payment - ACH credit\nL123:   * Satellite companies should click either\nL124:     * » Library of Congress Satellite Royalty Payment (credit card, debit card, or ACH debit payments) or\nL125:     * » Library of Congress Satellite Royalty Payment - ACH credit\nL126:   * DART manufacturers or importers should click either\nL127:     * »Library of Congress DART Royalty Payment (credit card, debit card, or ACH debit payments) or\nL128:     * » Library of Congress DART Royalty Payment - ACH credit\nL129: A cite3†tutorial slide deck details the full process.\nL130: ###### Related Information\nL131: \nL132: For other payment options, see How to Make Statutory License Royalty EFT Payments Using Pay.gov (cite2†Circular 74 ).\nL133: \nL134: [Button: Close]\nL135: # Filing Options for Cable Statements of Account for the 2026/1 Accounting Period\nL136: \nL137: Starting July 1, 2025, the Licensing Section will mandate electronic filing for all cable and satellite statements of account. To file electronically, follow the below instructions:\nL138: \nL139: Submit a statement of account via email to cite55†[email protected] , using either the Office’s Excel template (cite56†SA1-2E short form or cite57†SA3E long form ).\nL140:   * Please attach a completed cite58†remittance advice with the statement of account.\nL141:   * Please sign the form using an electronic or “s-signature” (for example, /s/ John Smith) and submit it in its native Excel format.\nL142:   * FILE SIZE LIMITATION: cite59†[email protected] has a file size limitation of 20 MB for each submission. Submit one email for each statement of account. The Office may not be able to accept multiple or batch statement of account filings in one email.\nL143: For more information or assistance, please email cite60†[email protected] .\nL144: \nL145: [Button: Close]\nL146: # Message regarding the Reading Room\nL147: \nL148: Beginning July 8, on-site services at the U.S. Copyright Office will open to the public by appointment only. To submit claims, request certified copies of certificates or copyright deposits, or conduct other in-person business, please make an appointment cite61†here†www.signupgenius.com . Note: Due to renovations, the Copyright Records Reading Room remains temporarily closed.)\nL149: \nL150: [Button: Close]\nL151: \nL152: About\nL153:   * cite62†Overview L154:   * cite63†Leadership L155:   * cite64†History and Education L156:   * cite65†Continuous Development L157:   * cite66†Small Claims L158:   * cite67†Annual Reports L159:   * cite68†Strategic Plans L160:   * cite69†IT Reports L161: \nL162: News\nL163: \nL164:   * cite70†Federal Register Notices L165:   * cite71†NewsNet L166:   * cite72†Events L167:   * cite73†Press/Media Information L168: \nL169: Opportunities\nL170: \nL171:   * cite74†Careers L172:   * cite75†Internships L173:   * cite7